## 4. ORDERING AT PROJECT SCALE

The steer was followed and NEXT's first act is now the right family: row 48 says the per-bead tracking cost is the lever, so working on the tracking loop is justified. But one thing scheduled behind M3 should come before it, and the project's own measurements say so.

**The split does not cut the per-bead cost. The kernel does.** M3 (L2-A1, 42 ops) moves tracking into its own loop behind a queue. At 15 beads the tracking work is already ~⅓ over the 11 ms frame period on both VIs (row 48). Decoupled, the acquisition loop stops dropping frames and the queue grows instead. The `Total Lost Frames` counter reads 0 and the experiment falls minutes behind. What reduces the work per frame is the project's own, already-built, bit-identical parallel kernel: sequential 8.15 ms vs CPU-parallel 2.43 ms at 5 beads (INDEX row 15), 10,043/10,043 frames identical (row 12), drop-in at the kernel's own connector pane (rows 17, 40), and "calling it directly at the main VI's single call site costs zero" (row 18). The main VI runs its tracking For loop with parallelism OFF on all 17 loops (row 30). The user wrote this lever down themselves: "the bead tracking and analysis code is performed by sequential for loop, which might turn into parallel looping" (`project-description.md:31`).

So the order at project scale should be: kernel swap on a copy of S1 (one call-site repoint, existing verified subVI), then row-48's driver at 15 picks, 120 s. If lost frames fall from ~⅓ toward 0, the user has a usable file this week and M3 becomes the second lever. If they do not, M3 is confirmed and nothing is lost but one cycle. M9 (GPU) stays after both, as the user asked.

Two small things also belong ahead of L2-A1 and cost almost nothing: the D-05 camera setting inside the new VI (`decisions_pending.json:121`), and the plain-language description the project owes the user (§5).

